[{:db/id 17592186221174 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={y}^{2}(t)-x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above." :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $\\mathbf{f}(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ {(\\frac{x}{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4x & =0 \\\\ x(x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y{:}$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{0}{2} = 0.$\n\n- For $x=4,$ we get $y = \\dfrac{4}{2} = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n![](images/82/82b6d431638e3abb1c1aebf818f3fbe9f2eb92c7232a3b83c93dc6cb4e563c91.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186417211 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{3}(t)+y(t)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y+1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+1=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+y+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+(-x-1)+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-x & =0 \\\\ x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ x(x-1)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\text{ }0,\\text{ }1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=-0-1=-1.$\n\n- For $x=1,$ we get $y=-1-1=-2.$\n\n- For $x=-1,$ we get $y=-(-1)-1=0.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311616 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)={x}^{2}(t)-2y(t)-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives all equilibrium points of the system of differential equations above?" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y-4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2y-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2(-x-2)-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+4-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\text{ }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=-0-2=-2.$\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=-(-2)-2=0.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
